<h2>Step 1: Get your above-the-fold content right</h2>

<p>"Above the fold" refers to everything visible on the screen before a visitor scrolls. It comes from newspaper design, where the most important headlines were placed above the physical fold of the paper. On a landing page, this is the most critical real estate you have.</p>

<p>Visitors decide within three to five seconds whether to stay or leave. That decision is made entirely based on what they see above the fold. So what needs to be there?</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>A clear, benefit-driven headline</strong> that tells the visitor what is in it for them</li>
  <li><strong>A supporting subheadline</strong> that adds context or specificity</li>
  <li><strong>A hero image or short video</strong> that reinforces the message visually</li>
  <li><strong>A visible call-to-action button</strong> so visitors can act immediately</li>
</ul>

<p>That is it. No mission statement. No company history. No navigation menu pulling attention in six different directions. Strip everything back to the essentials. If a visitor can look at your page for five seconds and clearly understand what you offer, why it matters to them, and what they should do next, you have done your job above the fold.</p>

<blockquote><p>If someone cannot understand your offer and find your CTA within five seconds, your above-the-fold content needs reworking. Clarity beats cleverness every time.</p></blockquote>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Step 2: Write headlines that actually convert</h2>

<p>Your headline is the single most important piece of copy on the page. Research consistently shows that eight out of ten visitors will read a headline, but only two out of ten will continue reading the body copy. If the headline does not hook them, nothing else matters.</p>

<p>So what makes a headline convert? Three things: specificity, benefit focus, and clarity.</p>

<h3>Be specific</h3>

<p>"Grow your business" is vague and forgettable. "Get 3x more leads from your website in 90 days" is specific. It gives the reader a concrete picture of what they will gain. Specificity builds credibility because it shows you have actually measured results rather than making general promises.</p>

<p>Numbers work particularly well in headlines. "5 ways to reduce no-shows by 40%" is more compelling than "reduce no-shows at your business." The brain responds to specifics because they feel real and tangible.</p>

<h3>Lead with the benefit</h3>

<p>"Our advanced scheduling platform features AI-powered automation" describes what you built. The visitor does not care. "Never lose another booking to a missed follow-up" describes what the visitor gets. That is the difference. Always lead with the outcome, not the feature.</p>

<p>A useful formula: start with the result the customer wants, then connect it to what you provide. "Fill your calendar with qualified leads" is benefit-first. "Advanced lead generation software" is feature-first. The benefit version wins every time.</p>

<h3>Keep it short and clear</h3>

<p>Aim for ten words or fewer in your main headline. Use a subheadline underneath for additional detail. The headline grabs attention. The subheadline gives enough context to keep them scrolling.</p>

<p>For example:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Headline:</strong> "Stop chasing leads. Let them come to you."</li>
  <li><strong>Subheadline:</strong> "Our automated follow-up system turns website visitors into booked appointments, without you lifting a finger."</li>
</ul>

