<h2>Step 5: Avoid the most common landing page mistakes</h2>

<p>Even well-intentioned landing pages can underperform because of a few avoidable errors. Here are the ones we see most often.</p>

<p><strong>Too many CTAs pulling in different directions.</strong> Your landing page should have one goal and one type of CTA. If you are asking visitors to book a call, download a guide, follow you on Instagram, and subscribe to your newsletter all on the same page, you are splitting their attention and diluting your conversion rate. Pick one action. Remove everything else.</p>

<p><strong>Walls of text with no visual breaks.</strong> Large blocks of unbroken text are intimidating on a screen, especially on mobile. Use short paragraphs, bullet points, subheadings, and images to break up the content and make it scannable. Most visitors will not read every word. They will scan for the information that matters to them. Make that easy.</p>

<p><strong>Slow load times.</strong> If your page takes more than three seconds to load, you will lose a significant portion of your visitors before they even see your content. Compress images, minimise unnecessary scripts, and avoid heavy animations or auto-playing video files. A fast, simple page will outperform a beautiful, slow one every single time.</p>

<p><strong>Leaving the navigation menu on the page.</strong> A landing page is not part of your website. It is a standalone conversion tool. If you leave your standard website navigation at the top, you are giving visitors a way to wander off to your About page, your blog, or your services page instead of taking the action you want. Remove navigation. Remove footer links. Keep the page focused.</p>

<p><strong>Ignoring the thank-you page.</strong> What happens after someone converts? If you just show a generic "thanks for submitting" message, you are missing an opportunity. Use the confirmation page to set expectations ("We will call you within 24 hours"), offer a secondary action ("While you wait, check out these resources"), or deepen the relationship. The moment after conversion is when engagement is highest.</p>

<h2>Step 6: Optimise for mobile first</h2>

<p>More than half of all web traffic comes from mobile devices. If you are driving traffic from social media ads, that percentage could be 70% or higher. Your landing page must work flawlessly on a phone. This is not a nice-to-have. It is the baseline.</p>

<p>Here is what mobile optimisation means in practice:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Large, tappable buttons.</strong> Your CTA button should be big enough to tap easily with a thumb. If it is too small or too close to other elements, visitors will either miss it or tap the wrong thing.</li>
  <li><strong>Readable text without zooming.</strong> Use a base font size of at least 16 pixels on mobile. If visitors have to pinch and zoom to read your content, they will leave.</li>
  <li><strong>Forms that work with thumbs.</strong> Use appropriate input types (email keyboard for email fields, number pad for phone fields) and make form fields tall enough to tap accurately. Auto-capitalisation, auto-correct, and smart defaults make forms feel easier to complete.</li>
  <li><strong>No horizontal scrolling.</strong> If any element on your page extends beyond the screen width, the experience breaks. Check every section on a real device.</li>
  <li><strong>Fast load times on mobile networks.</strong> Mobile connections are often slower than desktop. Compress images aggressively and defer non-essential scripts. Test your page speed using Google PageSpeed Insights and aim for a mobile score above 90.</li>
</ul>

<p>Test your landing page on your own phone before you publish it. Then test it on a friend's phone with a different screen size. What looks perfect on a desktop can be completely broken on mobile, and you will not know unless you check.</p>

<h2>Step 7: Use A/B testing to improve over time</h2>

<p>Your first landing page will not be your best one. A/B testing (also called split testing) is how you improve it. The concept is simple: create two versions of your page with one element changed, send equal traffic to both, and see which one performs better.</p>

<p>The key is to test one thing at a time. If you change your headline, your image, and your button colour all at once, you will not know which change made the difference.</p>

<h3>What to test first</h3>

<p>Start with the elements that have the biggest impact on conversions:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Headlines.</strong> Test two different headlines against each other. This is almost always where the biggest gains come from.</li>
  <li><strong>CTA button text and placement.</strong> Try different wording ("Book a call" vs "Get started free") and different positions on the page.</li>
  <li><strong>Form length.</strong> Test a three-field form against a two-field form to see if reducing friction increases submissions.</li>
  <li><strong>Social proof placement.</strong> Move testimonials higher or lower on the page and measure the impact.</li>
  <li><strong>Hero image or video.</strong> Test whether a product image, a team photo, or a short video performs best.</li>
</ol>

<h3>How long to run tests</h3>

<p>You need enough traffic to get a statistically meaningful result. As a rough guideline, aim for at least 200 to 300 visitors to each version before drawing conclusions. If you are sending 50 visitors a day, that means running each test for about two weeks.</p>

<p>Do not make snap judgements after a day or two. Small sample sizes produce unreliable results. Be patient, let the data accumulate, and then make your change based on what the numbers actually tell you.</p>

<p>You do not need expensive testing software to get started. Many <a href="/blog/landing-pages-that-convert">landing page builders</a> include basic A/B testing tools, and even manually swapping content every few days and comparing results in your analytics is better than never testing at all.</p>

<blockquote><p>The businesses that consistently improve their landing pages are the ones that test, measure, and iterate. One small improvement each month adds up to dramatically better results over a year.</p></blockquote>

<h2>Putting it all together</h2>

<p>Building a high-converting landing page is not about design tricks or secret formulas. It is about clarity, focus, and removing friction. Start with a headline that speaks to the visitor's problem. Back it up with social proof that builds trust. Make the CTA obvious and specific. Keep the page fast and mobile-friendly. Then test and improve based on real data.</p>

<p>The best landing pages are simple. They have one message, one offer, and one action. Everything on the page supports that single goal. Everything that does not support it gets removed.</p>

<p>If you are building out your broader marketing system, connect your landing page to your <a href="/blog/sales-funnel-small-business">sales funnel</a> and your <a href="/blog/marketing-automation-getting-started">marketing automation</a>. A landing page captures the lead. Your follow-up system turns that lead into a customer. The two work together, and the businesses that connect them properly see the best results.</p>

